import Native

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The scans, natively - the bulk operations over the string's own units.
   ==========================================================================================

   `Text.yana` beside this is the tier's documentation and everything both targets share;
   `Text.js.yana` is the host's answer to the same names. What is here is the set whose two
   implementations have nothing in common (Analysis-Modules.md §2.5), and natively every one of them
   is a vector loop over the string's bytes.

   **A string is a container of code units, and saying so is what vectorizes it.** The instance below
   is that sentence; `indexOf` and `occurrences` over it are already the vector search - a lanewise
   compare against the splatted unit and a `firstSet` over the hit mask, sixteen or thirty-two units
   at a time - so the two simplest functions here are one line each and inherit every improvement the
   bulk operations get.

   The rest walk the units directly rather than through `vectorsMasked`, and the reason is that each
   of them starts somewhere other than the beginning or ends somewhere other than the end: a search
   from an offset, a search from the far end, a comparison of two windows at once. `allUnits` is what
   makes that a container question rather than a pointer question - a `[CodeUnit]` over part of
   the string, rooted in it, with the vector transfers `Container.yana` declares over one.

   **The tail read is safe and the tail write is not.** `loadVectorTail` overreads by up to a
   vector's width, which Design-Vector §5.2's guarantee covers for storage the language allocated -
   and a string's run is exactly that, a literal's included. There is no store counterpart and there
   must not be, so every loop here that *writes* stops a vector short of the end and finishes its
   tail one unit at a time.
-}
instance Chunked(String, CodeUnit):
    iter fn chunks(self: 'String) -> '[CodeUnit]:
        yield allUnits(self)

{-
   The string's units, as a container - the private primitive every scan below is written over.

   A `[CodeUnit]` over the whole string - the two words of the string's own view, restated as the
   array's - rooted in it by the `return` marker, with the vector transfers `Container.yana`
   declares over one. `ArrayView` is Core's to build, which is why this is here and not in `Native`
   beside `units`. A scan that wants *part* of the string takes its
   window out of this with `slice` at the call site, which is now a preference rather than the
   compiler limitation it used to be: a borrow reaching a `return` position through *two* calls -
   `elements` and then `slice` - lost its root on the way, and the checker refused the second
   function with "rooted in storage the caller does not own". That is fixed (`resolveViewRoots` in
   `analyze_provenance.cpp`, with `test/resolve/SliceRoot.yana` over both halves), so a windowing
   helper may be written where one earns its place. None does here: the windows below each want
   different bounds, and a helper would carry the same `@inline` this does, so the two spellings
   would be the same code.

   **A scan that wants the whole string takes no slice at all**, and six of them used to:
   `slice(allUnits(text), 0, length(text))` is the identity, and the loops it fed were bounded by
   their own `count` rather than by the container's length, so the slice was a call that computed
   what it was handed. `bench/text` prices it at about 1.2 ns per call on the short operations and at
   28% of `spansOn`'s line walk, which is one `findAscii` per part. The last two were the ones whose
   whole-string operand is named something else - `startsWith`'s *prefix* and `String.find`'s
   *needle* - which is why they outlived the first four.

   **`@inline`, and it is worth the measurement rather than the reflex.** This is two loads and a
   descriptor; left to the inliner's own budget it was a `call` in every scan in this file, with the
   `Flat` returned through the caller's stack slot. See `loweredUnits` below, which was the same
   story a second time and is where most of the difference was.
-}
@inline fn allUnits(text: source'String) -> source'[CodeUnit] =
    ArrayView {items: units(text), length: length(text)}

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Searching for one unit
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   One vector pass: a lane-wise compare against the splatted unit, and `firstSet` over the hit mask.
   `from` costs nothing - it is where the window starts, so a search that resumes is the same loop
   over a shorter container rather than a loop with a test in it.
-}
pub fn findAscii(self: String, unit: CodeUnit, from: Size = 0) -> Maybe(Size):
    let count = length(self)
    let &begin = from
    if begin < 0 then begin = 0
    if begin >= count then return Nothing

    return match indexOf(slice(allUnits(self), begin, count), unit):
        Just(at) -> Just(begin + at)
        Nothing -> Nothing

-- The same pass with a mask count instead of a first-set, so a count is one traversal rather than a
-- search restarted at every hit.
pub fn countAscii(self: String, unit: CodeUnit) -> Int = occurrences(self, unit)

{-
   The last one, which is the same compare walked from the far end.

   **The blocks are walked backwards and the lanes inside the hit block forwards**, and that split is
   what lets this be written with the mask operations that exist. `firstSet` answers the *lowest* set
   lane and there is no `lastSet`; running one over a reversed vector would need a cross-lane permute
   this backend does not have at every width. So the vector half answers "is the last unit in this
   block", which is one `any`, and the block that says yes is re-walked one unit at a time - at most a
   vector's width of comparisons, once per call.

   The head shorter than one vector is the same walk with no block in front of it, which is what the
   second loop is.
-}
pub fn findLastAscii(self: String, unit: CodeUnit) -> Maybe(Size):
    let count = length(self)
    if count <= 0 then return Nothing

    let window = allUnits(self)
    let width = vectorBytes
    let sought = splat(unit) :: Vec(CodeUnit)
    let &at = count - width

    while at >= 0:
        if any(loadVector(window, at) .== sought):
            let &j = at + width - 1

            while j >= at:
                if window[j] == unit then return Just(j)
                j = j - 1

        at = at - width

    let &head = at + width
    while head > 0:
        head = head - 1
        if window[head] == unit then return Just(head)

    return Nothing

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Searching for any of a set of units - `Text.h`'s `findChar(StringView, Buffer<char>)`.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   The delimiter scan a parser is written over: the next comma *or* newline, the next quote or
   backslash, the end of a token. The C++ this replaces writes the loop out five times, once per
   candidate count from one to five, because the splats have to be in registers and a count known at
   compile time is what puts them there.

   **One membership test per block, whatever the set** - `memberOf` over a `ByteSet` built once in
   front of the loop (Analysis-Avx512.md §3.4): two `pshufb`, a shift, two `and` and a comparison
   per block, where the loop this replaced did a splat, a compare and an `or` for each candidate.
   Measured over a megabyte at every level, the set costs the same at one unit as at six and beats the
   candidate walk at every count from one - 1.3x at one unit and 3.7x at six at `sse4`, 1.1x and
   2.9x at `avx2`, 1.2x and 4.4x at `avx512` (test/bench/avx512/findings.md §9). The copies of the C++
   loop per candidate count that this was written to avoid are not needed either.

   **A unit at or above 128 in the set is ignored**, which is the set's own rule and what the name
   says: no unit of a multi-unit UTF-8 sequence is ASCII, so a search for one could only ever find
   the middle of a code point. `Text.js.yana` states the same.

   **The tail is one more block** read past the end on the window's padding, with the lanes past the
   end masked off before `any` - where it used to be a unit at a time against the set, which
   Analysis-Avx512.md §4.13 found to be a policy rather than a requirement.
-}
pub fn findAsciiAny(self: String, units: [CodeUnit], from: Size = 0) -> Maybe(Size):
    let count = length(self)
    let &begin = from
    if begin < 0 then begin = 0
    if begin >= count || length(units) == 0 then return Nothing

    let window = slice(allUnits(self), begin, count)
    let span = length(window)
    let width = vectorBytes
    let set = byteSet(units)
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i + width <= span:
        let hits = memberOf(bitcast(loadVector(window, i)) :: Vec(U8), set)
        if any(hits) then return Just(begin + i + (firstSet(hits) :: Size))
        i = i + width

    if i < span:
        let live = maskUpTo(truncate(span - i) :: Int) :: Mask(U8)
        let hits = and(memberOf(bitcast(loadVectorTail(window, i)) :: Vec(U8), set), live)
        if any(hits) then return Just(begin + i + (firstSet(hits) :: Size))

    return Nothing

{-
   And the inverse - the first unit that is *not* one of them, which is `findCharInverse`.

   The same mask, negated - and in the tail the live mask is applied *after* the negation, so that a
   lane past the end, which is no member of anything, cannot report itself as the miss.
-}
pub fn findAsciiNone(self: String, units: [CodeUnit], from: Size = 0) -> Maybe(Size):
    let count = length(self)
    let &begin = from
    if begin < 0 then begin = 0
    if begin >= count then return Nothing
    if length(units) == 0 then return Just(begin)

    let window = slice(allUnits(self), begin, count)
    let span = length(window)
    let width = vectorBytes
    let set = byteSet(units)
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i + width <= span:
        let misses = not(memberOf(bitcast(loadVector(window, i)) :: Vec(U8), set))
        if any(misses) then return Just(begin + i + (firstSet(misses) :: Size))
        i = i + width

    if i < span:
        let live = maskUpTo(truncate(span - i) :: Int) :: Mask(U8)
        let misses = and(not(memberOf(bitcast(loadVectorTail(window, i)) :: Vec(U8), set)), live)
        if any(misses) then return Just(begin + i + (firstSet(misses) :: Size))

    return Nothing

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Comparing two windows
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Whether two equal-length windows hold the same units, which is what `startsWith`, `endsWith` and
   the substring search all end in.

   **The comparison itself is `Native.compareMemory`'s walk** - whole registers under `all` and the
   remainder under one compare of the overreading loads, so a needle shorter than one vector is a
   single pair of loads, a compare and a movemask rather than a walk, and one of eight units or fewer
   is two scalar reads a side. The count is the *left* window's length, which is what makes the
   asymmetry at `startsWith` below correct.

   Both windows are inside runs the language allocated, so both tail reads are covered by
   Design-Vector §5.2 the same way `vectors` is - which is the guarantee `compareMemory` asks its
   callers for and is why it takes two addresses rather than two slices.

   **`Native.sameUnits` rather than `compareMemory(...) == EQ`**, which it was until J175 on the
   reading that an `Ordering` where a `Bool` is wanted costs nothing because the arm that names one is
   taken at most once. That is true of the time and not of the bytes: every inlined copy carried the
   three-way compare of the differing lane, and a needle of eight units or fewer is two scalar reads
   a side there instead of a register.
-}
fn unitsEqual(left: [CodeUnit], right: [CodeUnit]) -> Bool =
    sameUnits(left.items, right.items, length(left))

{-
   The length test first, which is what makes the comparison's bound safe as well as what makes the
   common answer free.

   **The prefix is not sliced and the text is**, and the asymmetry is the point: `wanted` *is* the
   prefix's length, so `slice(allUnits(prefix), 0, wanted)` computes what it was handed and was
   costing a call to do it - the same identity §2 of `test/bench/text/findings.md` found in the four
   scans. The text side is a real window and keeps its slice, since what `unitsEqual` reads off its
   left operand is the count both sides are compared over.
-}
pub fn startsWith(text: String, prefix: String) -> Bool:
    let wanted = length(prefix)
    if wanted > length(text) then return False

    return unitsEqual(slice(allUnits(text), 0, wanted), allUnits(prefix))

-- And from the other end, on the same terms.
pub fn endsWith(text: String, suffix: String) -> Bool:
    let wanted = length(suffix)
    let count = length(text)
    if wanted > count then return False

    return unitsEqual(slice(allUnits(text), count - wanted, count), allUnits(suffix))

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Searching for a whole needle
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   **Two units of the needle are tested at once, and that is the whole algorithm.** A block of the
   text is compared against the needle's first unit and the block starting `inner - 1` units later
   against its last, and a position can only start a match where both hold. One vector loop, two
   loads, two compares and an `and` per block, and no preprocessing of the needle at all.

   The alternative - scan for the first unit, verify the rest at each hit - is what `Text.h`'s
   `findString` does and it is the shape this took first. It is `1/A` of the positions per candidate
   where the text has `A` distinct units, so a needle beginning with a common letter over a
   sixteen-letter alphabet re-enters the scan every sixteen units and the re-entry is most of the
   cost. Testing both ends squares that: **1/A² candidates**, which over the benchmark's corpus is
   one in two hundred and fifty-six rather than one in sixteen. Measured on `bench/text`, it is the
   difference between 2.2 GB/s and the rest of this file's throughput.

   A block that survives the filter visits its candidates and nothing else: `bits` of the candidate
   mask, a trailing-zero count for the next one and `hits and (hits - 1)` to clear it, so each is
   one `unitsEqual` and there is no re-reading of the block. ~~The block used to be re-walked one
   position at a time~~, when there was no way to clear a mask's lowest set lane - `width`
   comparisons on one block in `A²/width`, which is a constant per unit only while the width is. At
   `avx512`'s sixty-four bytes it doubled, and was the whole of this function running 1.19x slower
   at 512 bits than at 256 (test/bench/avx512/findings.md §10).

   Boyer-Moore or a two-way search would beat this on a long needle against adversarial text, and
   the tables they need cost more than the scan they save on the strings this is actually asked
   about - switches, lines, paths and headers. That is the ruling Analysis-Library.md §2.2 makes for
   the family, and it is why there is no preprocessing step here to skip when the needle is short.

   **An empty needle is found at `from`**, which is what makes `String.contains(text, "")` true and
   what every string library in use answers. `String.replace` and `splitOnText` each refuse an empty
   needle before they reach this, because "found everywhere" is not a thing either of those loops can
   terminate on.

   Every whole-block load is inside the haystack: the last block the loop reads starts `inner - 1`
   units after the last candidate position, which is `length - width`, so the read ends exactly at
   the end. That is why the loop is bounded by the number of *candidate positions* rather than by
   the length. **The remaining candidates are one more block on the same terms**, which is what the
   scalar walk that used to be here has become: both loads start at live units, so `loadVectorTail`
   covers them, and `maskUpTo(truncate(starts - i) :: Int)` excludes the lanes whose start is past
   the last candidate. A hit still needs its scalar `unitsEqual`, which is why this is a filter and
   not an answer - but so does a hit in a whole block, and the filter is the same two compares
   either way.
-}
pub fn String.find(haystack: String, needle: String, from: Size = 0) -> Maybe(Size):
    let outer = length(haystack)
    let inner = length(needle)

    let &at = from
    if at < 0 then at = 0
    if at > outer then at = outer
    if inner == 0 then return Just(at)
    if at + inner > outer then return Nothing

    -- The needle is not sliced and the haystack is, which is the asymmetry `startsWith` above states
    -- and the same reason: `inner` *is* the needle's length, so a slice here computes what it was
    -- handed. `unitsEqual` reads its count off the *left* operand, so even this one's length field
    -- was never looked at - the call was the whole of the cost.
    let sought = allUnits(needle)
    let firstUnit = String.unit(needle, 0)
    let lastUnit = String.unit(needle, inner - 1)

    let window = slice(allUnits(haystack), at, outer)
    let starts = length(window) - inner + 1
    let width = vectorBytes
    let last = starts - width

    let firstLane = splat(firstUnit) :: Vec(CodeUnit)
    let lastLane = splat(lastUnit) :: Vec(CodeUnit)
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i <= last:
        let heads = loadVector(window, i) .== firstLane
        let tails = loadVector(window, i + inner - 1) .== lastLane

        let &hits = bits(and(heads, tails))

        while hits != 0:
            let j = i + (trailingZeros(hits) :: Size)
            if unitsEqual(slice(window, j, j + inner), sought) then return Just(at + j)
            hits = hits `and` (hits - 1)

        i = i + width

    -- The remaining candidates are one more block of each end, read past the window on the run's
    -- padding and masked to the positions that can really start a match - §4.13's fourth row. Both
    -- loads begin at a live unit: the last candidate is `starts - 1` and its last unit is the last
    -- unit of the window, so `i + inner - 1` is inside it for every `i` below `starts`. What the
    -- live mask excludes is a lane whose *start* is past the last candidate, which is the only way
    -- a padding byte could name one.
    if i < starts:
        let live = maskUpTo(truncate(starts - i) :: Int) :: Mask(CodeUnit)
        let heads = loadVectorTail(window, i) .== firstLane
        let tails = loadVectorTail(window, i + inner - 1) .== lastLane

        let &hits = bits(and(and(heads, tails), live))

        while hits != 0:
            let j = i + (trailingZeros(hits) :: Size)
            if unitsEqual(slice(window, j, j + inner), sought) then return Just(at + j)
            hits = hits `and` (hits - 1)

    return Nothing

{-
   The last occurrence, as one forward pass rather than a backward search.

   Each `String.find` resumes where the previous match ended, so the whole loop reads the text once -
   the same work a backward scan would do, without a second implementation of the candidate search
   and its windows. What it costs against a true backward search is the case where the answer is near
   the end of a long text and there are many matches before it, which is not the shape `endsWith`-like
   questions have.
-}
pub fn String.findLast(haystack: String, needle: String) -> Maybe(Size):
    let outer = length(haystack)
    let inner = length(needle)
    if inner == 0 then return Just(outer)
    if inner > outer then return Nothing

    let &best = Nothing :: Maybe(Size)
    let &at = 0 :: Size

    while String.find(haystack, needle, at) is Just(hit):
        best = Just(hit)
        at = hit + 1

    return best

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The ASCII case tier
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   `Text_case_sysv_x64.s` in the C++ is hand-written assembly for exactly this, and here it is a
   lanewise `select`: the letters are one range, so the test is two compares and an `and`, and the
   change is one add of the constant 32 the two ASCII alphabets are apart. Nothing decodes, and
   nothing has to - no unit inside a multi-unit encoding is in `A`-`Z` or `a`-`z` in either UTF-8 or
   UTF-16.

   **The range test is signed, and that is what makes it two instructions.** A code unit is unsigned,
   so `unit >= 65 && unit <= 90` over `Vec(CodeUnit)` asks for two *unsigned* byte comparisons, and
   the only byte comparison this target has is signed - so each of them becomes a bias and a compare.
   Read as `Vec(I8)` the same test is `> 64 && < 91` with the comparison the machine has, and it is
   still correct for every unit: a byte above 0x7f is negative read that way, so it fails `> 64` and
   is left alone, which is exactly what a continuation byte or a UTF-16 high unit must have happen to
   it.

   **One pass, source to target**, rather than a copy followed by a transform in place. The copy
   would be `copyMemory`'s ladder and faster per byte than anything a loop here does, but it makes
   the whole operation read and write the units twice - and measured on `bench/text` that is where
   the time goes, not in the select.

   The length is set before the loop because the loop *is* what fills the string: `ofCapacity` gives
   a run and no units, and the store loop needs a destination whose length is already what it will
   be. Nothing reads the string in between.

   **The remainder is one more block**, read past the end on the run's padding (Design-Vector §5.2)
   and written with `storeUpTo`, which stops at the count: a write past the end of the run would be
   the destruction of whatever follows it, where a read past it is only unspecified bytes. It used to
   be a unit at a time, and one masked block is faster at every length from three units up at every
   level - 2.1x at three and 7-11x from fifteen at `avx512`, where `storeUpTo` is one instruction,
   and 1.7-11x through the ladder below it (test/bench/avx512/findings.md §9). The count is taken
   `and (width - 1)`, which changes nothing - it is below the width - and says so to the backend,
   which then needs no clamp in front of the mask.
-}
pub fn toLowerAscii(text: String) -> String:
    let count = length(text)
    let &out = String.ofCapacity(count)
    if count <= 0 then return out

    out.units.length = truncate(count) :: Count

    let source = units(text)
    let target = out.units.run.items
    let width = vectorBytes
    let last = count - width

    let low = splat(64) :: Vec(I8)
    let high = splat(91) :: Vec(I8)
    let bias = splat(32) :: Vec(I8)
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i <= last:
        let block = bitcast(vectorAt(source + i) :: Vec(CodeUnit)) :: Vec(I8)
        let letters = and(block .> low, block .< high)
        setVectorAt(target + i, bitcast(select(letters, block + bias, block)) :: Vec(CodeUnit))
        i = i + width

    if i < count:
        let block = bitcast(vectorPast(source + i) :: Vec(CodeUnit)) :: Vec(I8)
        let letters = and(block .> low, block .< high)
        storeUpTo(target + i, truncate((count - i) `and` (width - 1)) :: Int,
                  bitcast(select(letters, block + bias, block)) :: Vec(CodeUnit))

    return out

pub fn toUpperAscii(text: String) -> String:
    let count = length(text)
    let &out = String.ofCapacity(count)
    if count <= 0 then return out

    out.units.length = truncate(count) :: Count

    let source = units(text)
    let target = out.units.run.items
    let width = vectorBytes
    let last = count - width

    let low = splat(96) :: Vec(I8)
    let high = splat(123) :: Vec(I8)
    let bias = splat(32) :: Vec(I8)
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i <= last:
        let block = bitcast(vectorAt(source + i) :: Vec(CodeUnit)) :: Vec(I8)
        let letters = and(block .> low, block .< high)
        setVectorAt(target + i, bitcast(select(letters, block - bias, block)) :: Vec(CodeUnit))
        i = i + width

    if i < count:
        let block = bitcast(vectorPast(source + i) :: Vec(CodeUnit)) :: Vec(I8)
        let letters = and(block .> low, block .< high)
        storeUpTo(target + i, truncate((count - i) `and` (width - 1)) :: Int,
                  bitcast(select(letters, block - bias, block)) :: Vec(CodeUnit))

    return out

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   A text with one ASCII unit, or the six ASCII spaces, taken out - delimiter and whitespace
   stripping, Analysis-Avx512.md §3.1.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   `toLowerAscii`'s loop with `compress` where the case change was: a block is read, the units that
   stay are packed to the bottom, and the whole register is stored where the text written so far
   ends. The lanes past the count are the next store's to overwrite, so nothing zeroes them.

   Removing an ASCII unit cannot break a code point, because no byte of a multi-byte UTF-8 sequence
   is below 128 - so the answer is valid UTF-8 whenever the text was, and the loop reads bytes without
   caring which is which. The spaces are `isSpace`'s six, compared as signed bytes so that every
   byte of a longer sequence is negative and outside `\t`..`\r` without a third comparison.

   The store at `kept` writes a register's width, and `kept <= i` keeps it inside the capacity the
   answer was made with. The tail is `toLowerAscii`'s: one block read past the end, its live lanes
   the only ones kept, and the survivors written with `storeUpTo`, which stops at their count - and
   since that count is below the width, `and (width - 1)` says so for nothing.
-}
pub fn removeAscii(text: String, unit: CodeUnit) -> String:
    let count = length(text)
    let &out = String.ofCapacity(count)
    if count <= 0 then return out

    let source = units(text)
    let target = out.units.run.items
    let width = vectorBytes
    let last = count - width

    let sought = splat(unit) :: Vec(CodeUnit)
    let &i = 0 :: Size
    let &kept = 0 :: Size

    while i <= last:
        let block = vectorAt(source + i) :: Vec(CodeUnit)
        let {packed, survivors} = compress(block, block .!= sought)
        setVectorAt(target + kept, packed)
        kept = kept + (survivors :: Size)
        i = i + width

    if i < count:
        let block = vectorPast(source + i) :: Vec(CodeUnit)
        let live = maskUpTo(truncate(count - i) :: Int) :: Mask(CodeUnit)
        let {packed, survivors} = compress(block, and(block .!= sought, live))
        storeUpTo(target + kept, survivors `and` (lanes(block) - 1), packed)
        kept = kept + (survivors :: Size)

    out.units.length = truncate(kept) :: Count
    return out

pub fn removeAsciiSpace(text: String) -> String:
    let count = length(text)
    let &out = String.ofCapacity(count)
    if count <= 0 then return out

    let source = units(text)
    let target = out.units.run.items
    let width = vectorBytes
    let last = count - width

    let space = splat(32) :: Vec(I8)
    let belowTab = splat(8) :: Vec(I8)
    let aboveReturn = splat(14) :: Vec(I8)
    let &i = 0 :: Size
    let &kept = 0 :: Size

    while i <= last:
        let block = vectorAt(source + i) :: Vec(CodeUnit)
        let signed = bitcast(block) :: Vec(I8)
        let spaces = or(signed .== space, and(signed .> belowTab, signed .< aboveReturn))
        let {packed, survivors} = compress(signed, not(spaces))
        setVectorAt(target + kept, bitcast(packed) :: Vec(CodeUnit))
        kept = kept + (survivors :: Size)
        i = i + width

    if i < count:
        let signed = bitcast(vectorPast(source + i) :: Vec(CodeUnit)) :: Vec(I8)
        let spaces = or(signed .== space, and(signed .> belowTab, signed .< aboveReturn))
        let live = maskUpTo(truncate(count - i) :: Int) :: Mask(I8)
        let {packed, survivors} = compress(signed, and(not(spaces), live))
        storeUpTo(target + kept, survivors `and` (lanes(signed) - 1), bitcast(packed) :: Vec(CodeUnit))
        kept = kept + (survivors :: Size)

    out.units.length = truncate(kept) :: Count
    return out

{-
   Case-insensitive equality, which is the fold above applied to both operands before the compare -
   `Text.h`'s `equalsCaseString`, and the reason it is worth having as one function is that the
   alternative is two whole-string allocations at every call site that wanted to ask.

   The length test first, exactly as `Eq(String)` does, and for both of its reasons: it is what makes
   the loop's bound safe and it is what makes the common answer free. A case fold that changed unit
   counts could not do that, which is one more thing the ASCII restriction buys.
-}
{-
   The fold itself, over one register - two compares, an `and` and a masked add.

   **`@inline` for the reason `allUnits` carries one**, and here it was most of what the case tier
   cost: five vector instructions behind a call is a `movups` of the argument into the callee's slot
   and another of the result back out, twice per block, on a function whose whole body is shorter
   than its calling sequence. `equalsAsciiCase` over 64 units was 14.4 ns with the call and 6.0 ns
   without, against Tritium's 4.8 - and the rest of the distance was the identity slice above.
-}
@inline fn loweredUnits(block: Vec(I8)) -> Vec(I8):
    let low = splat(64) :: Vec(I8)
    let high = splat(91) :: Vec(I8)
    let letters = and(block .> low, block .< high)

    return select(letters, block + (splat(32) :: Vec(I8)), block)

@inline fn signedUnits(from: [CodeUnit], at: Size) -> Vec(I8) =
    bitcast(loadVector(from, at)) :: Vec(I8)

-- And the same read past the end, for the block shorter than a register - `loadVectorTail`'s
-- overread, which Design-Vector §5.2 covers for a string's run. What makes the lanes it reads past
-- the end harmless is the caller's, and the two callers do it differently: `unitsEqual` masks them
-- into agreement, `compareAsciiCase` lets them disagree and tests the position it finds.
@inline fn signedUnitsTail(from: [CodeUnit], at: Size) -> Vec(I8) =
    bitcast(loadVectorTail(from, at)) :: Vec(I8)

pub fn equalsAsciiCase(left: String, right: String) -> Bool:
    let count = length(left)
    if count != length(right) then return False

    let a = allUnits(left)
    let b = allUnits(right)
    let width = vectorBytes
    let last = count - width
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i <= last:
        if !all(loweredUnits(signedUnits(a, i)) .== loweredUnits(signedUnits(b, i))) then return False
        i = i + width

    -- The remainder is `compareAsciiCase`'s tail arm asked for a `Bool` - one overreading load of
    -- each operand, one fold of each, and the *position* of the first disagreement tested against
    -- the length rather than a live mask built to suppress it. §4.13's second row, and the reason
    -- it is the position and not a mask is measured below: a `maskUpTo` here is a general register
    -- moved into a vector, a broadcast, a pooled `iota` and an unsigned bias, where this is a `cmp`.
    if i < count:
        let differ = not(loweredUnits(signedUnitsTail(a, i)) .== loweredUnits(signedUnitsTail(b, i)))
        if any(differ) && i + (firstSet(differ) :: Size) < count then return False

    return True

{-
   Case-insensitive *ordering*, which is the same fold asked a harder question: not whether the two
   agree but where the first place they do not is. `Text.yana` states the contract; this is the
   vector answer to it.

   **One iteration for every string short enough to be a name**, which is the whole reason this is
   not the scalar walk it used to be. The comparison a sorted list of identifiers asks for parts on a
   unit near the start, and the walk that was here read one unit of each operand per position - so
   eight units of shared prefix were eight loads, eight folds and sixteen branches. The tail read
   makes the same eight units one overreading load of each, one fold of each, one compare and one
   `pmovmskb`, because a block shorter than a register is exactly what `loadVectorTail` is for and
   *every* string under sixteen units is one. The setup that buys it is what `loweredUnits` already
   needs - two splats for the letter range and one for the bias - and those are three constants the
   loop does not reload.

   The scalar form's own argument was that a vector one would "fold whole blocks to find the block
   that differs and then walk it anyway", and that is the part that was wrong: `firstSet` over the
   difference mask names the position outright and there is no walk. What is re-read at that position
   is one unit of each operand, once per call.

   Measured on `bench/text`, at v2 and against `Tritium::compareCaseString`, which is the same
   algorithm in SSE2 intrinsics:

   | operands | scalar walk | here | Tritium |
   |---|---|---|---|
   | 8 units, parting at the sixth | 6.24 ns | **2.93 ns** | 2.36 ns |
   | 64 units, folding equal | 64.98 ns | **4.73 ns** | 5.43 ns |

   The eight-unit row is the one the change was made for and it is the smaller of the two numbers,
   which is worth saying plainly: the win at a name's length is about a factor of two, and the win at
   a line's length is more than a factor of ten.

   **The lanes past the end are left to disagree and the *position* is tested instead**, and that is
   the tail arm's `at < shared`. Forcing the padding into agreement is the other way to write it, and
   it costs a `maskUpTo` - which is `iota .< splat(n)` and therefore a general register moved into a
   vector, a broadcast, a pooled `iota`, the bias an unsigned lane comparison needs and the two
   exclusive-ors that apply it - where this costs one `cmp`, because `firstSet` answers the *lowest*
   set lane and a disagreement at or past `shared` means every live lane agreed. Measured both ways:
   **0.39 ns of the eight-unit row** (3.32 against 2.93) and nothing at all of the sixty-four-unit
   one, which is four whole registers and never reaches the tail arm.

   `unitsEqual` above takes the same shape now and for the same reason; it was the masked form until
   the measurement here said what that cost, and a sixteen-unit `startsWith` went from 2.55 ns to
   1.82 with it. `Tritium::compareCaseString` pays neither, because its `stringSegmentMask` is built
   in the movemask domain where a tail mask is a shift of an immediate.
-}
fn foldedUnitOrder(left: String, right: String, at: Size) -> Ordering:
    let a = toLowerUnit(left.unit(at))
    let b = toLowerUnit(right.unit(at))
    if a < b then return LT
    if a > b then return GT
    return EQ

pub fn compareAsciiCase(left: String, right: String) -> Ordering:
    let leftCount = length(left)
    let rightCount = length(right)
    let &shared = leftCount
    if rightCount < shared then shared = rightCount

    let a = allUnits(left)
    let b = allUnits(right)
    let width = vectorBytes
    let last = shared - width
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i <= last:
        let same = loweredUnits(signedUnits(a, i)) .== loweredUnits(signedUnits(b, i))
        let differ = not(same)
        if any(differ) then return foldedUnitOrder(left, right, i + (firstSet(differ) :: Size))
        i = i + width

    if i < shared:
        let same = loweredUnits(signedUnitsTail(a, i)) .== loweredUnits(signedUnitsTail(b, i))
        let differ = not(same)

        if any(differ):
            let at = i + (firstSet(differ) :: Size)
            if at < shared then return foldedUnitOrder(left, right, at)

    -- No position parted them, so the lengths do - the shorter string is the prefix and orders first.
    if leftCount < rightCount then return LT
    if leftCount > rightCount then return GT
    return EQ

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Whether every unit is ASCII
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   `isAllAscii`, and the one predicate here whose whole answer is a mask test: a unit is ASCII exactly
   when it is below 0x80, so this is one unsigned compare per block and no `firstSet` at all. It is
   what a caller asks before deciding whether the rest of this file's answers are the whole story
   about a text.
-}
pub fn isAllAscii(text: String) -> Bool:
    let count = length(text)
    if count <= 0 then return True

    let window = allUnits(text)
    let width = vectorBytes
    let last = count - width
    let limit = splat(127) :: Vec(CodeUnit)
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i <= last:
        if any(loadVector(window, i) .> limit) then return False
        i = i + width

    -- The remainder is one more block, read past the end on the run's padding and masked to the
    -- units that are really there - Analysis-Avx512.md §4.13's first row, and the one it measured
    -- before it was written: 4.64x at thirty-one bytes on AVX2 against the walk that was here.
    -- A one-byte remainder is the case that still favours the walk, and it is not worth a branch
    -- that would be mispredicted on every string whose length is not a multiple of the width.
    if i < count:
        let live = maskUpTo(truncate(count - i) :: Int) :: Mask(CodeUnit)
        if any(and(loadVectorTail(window, i) .> limit, live)) then return False

    return True
